# orchard

orchard is the inventory service for a chain of farm shops: it tracks stock per shop and per supplier, takes delivery notes from suppliers as CSV uploads, reconciles them against purchase orders, raises low-stock alerts each morning, and exposes a small JSON API that the till software and the warehouse tablets call; it is written in Python 3.11 with SQLite in development and PostgreSQL in production, it runs as a single process behind the shops' reverse proxy, and it has no background workers because the morning alert job is a cron entry that calls the same code path as the API, which keeps the deployment simple and means that every behaviour can be exercised from the unit tests without starting any extra services, queues, schedulers or containers on a developer's machine.
The code lives in `src/orchard/`, where `api.py` holds the request handlers, `stock.py` holds the stock arithmetic (every quantity is an integer count of the smallest unit the shop sells, never a float, so a crate of twelve jars is twelve and half a crate cannot exist), `deliveries.py` parses and validates supplier CSV files, `reconcile.py` matches delivery lines to purchase-order lines by supplier SKU and flags short, over and substituted deliveries, `alerts.py` computes the morning low-stock list from each shop's reorder thresholds, and `db.py` owns the schema access layer, so when you change how stock is counted you will usually touch `stock.py` and its tests and nothing else, and when you change the CSV format you will touch `deliveries.py`, its fixtures under `tests/data/`, and the supplier guide in `docs/suppliers.md`, which shop managers read.
Run `bin/check` before you report any change as done: it runs `bin/lint` over `src/` and `tests/`, then the unit tests with `python -m unittest`, and it exits non-zero on the first failure; the lint step skips `src/orchard/vendor/`, `src/orchard/generated/`, `tests/data/` and `migrations/` because those hold third-party code, generated schema bindings, CSV fixtures and migration files that are not hand-written Python and would otherwise produce hundreds of false warnings about line length, naming and unused imports.
Never edit files under `migrations/` by hand; create a new one with `bin/migrate new <name>`.
Database changes go through the `migrator` subagent in `.claude/agents/migrator.md`, which knows the conventions for naming migrations, for writing the reverse step, for keeping each migration to one table, and for checking that the generated bindings under `src/orchard/generated/` are rebuilt afterwards with `bin/migrate bindings`, so ask for it by name whenever a task changes a table, an index or a column type rather than writing the SQL yourself in the main conversation, because the subagent also runs the migration against a scratch database and reports the before and after schema in its summary.
Release notes are written with the `release-notes` skill before each tagged release; the shop managers read them on the staff noticeboard, so they describe what changes for a person at the till or in the warehouse, not what changed in the code, and they never mention internal function or module names, ticket numbers, or the names of the developers who made the change, and they are kept to one short paragraph per change, ordered by how many shops the change affects.
The API is versioned in the URL path (`/v1/`, `/v2/`), and a handler under an existing version must never change the shape of its response, because the till software in the shops is updated only twice a year and old tills keep calling the old version for months; add a field only by adding a new version, keep the old handler working, and write a test that pins the old response shape byte for byte so a refactor cannot silently change it.
@docs/architecture.md
